  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining cool today, with shower and \nthunderstorm chances returning late in the week and into the \nweekend.\n\nWith a broad 1026-mb surface anticyclone anchored over the \nnortheastern U.S., expect conditions to be fairly similar to \nyesterday/Monday. The one major difference will be the marked \nreduction in temperatures and humidity, as well as winds given \nweak gradients within the influence of this dome of high \npressure. Today's high temperatures are forecast to reach the \nmid 70s to low 80s (upper 60s to low 70s in the mountains). This\ncomes with continued low humidity with dew points mainly in the\nupper 40s to low 50s. Aside from some passing high cirrus, \nabundant sunshine is expected throughout the day. Light \nsoutheasterly gradients will favor an uptick in overnight \ntemperatures. While still cooler than recent days, most can \nanticipate lows in the mid 50s to low 60s (mildest along the \nI-95 urban areas as well as near the Potomac River and \nChesapeake Bay shorelines).\n\nBehind an exiting longwave trough that pushes into the Canadian\nMaritimes, mid/upper heights begin to build in earnest. An\nexpansive near 600-dm mid-level ridge initially over the \nsouthern U.S. will flex northward in time. These above average \nheights remain a fixture in the forecast through Thursday. This \nsupports a return of warmer temperatures along with building \nhumidity levels. Daily highs push back into the mid/upper 80s, \nlocally reaching the low 90s in the warm spots. The return of \nthe humidity also favors milder nights with widespread lows in \nthe 60s. Any shower and thunderstorm chances should hold off \nuntil Thursday given the stout ridge aloft quelling most \nprecipitation chances. Most models keep such a threat mainly \nwest of the Shenandoah Valley, especially closer to the \nAlleghenies.\n\nA high latitude trough digging across southeastern Canada should\nbe enough to nudge the upper ridge back toward the south-central\nU.S. This allows a cold front to push through early Friday\nwhich again brings a gradual cool down with decreasing humidity\ninto Saturday. However, this reprieve is somewhat brief as \nadditional disturbances in the atmosphere track toward the area \nlate in the weekend. The overall jet pattern turns a bit more \ncomplex as an array of features enter the picture. A broad \nupper low retrogrades across the Gulf. At the same time, a \nmyriad of shortwaves will eject across the central U.S. toward \nthe Mississippi Valley. How these impulses behave along with \nwhere surface features reside will dictate any local weather \nimpacts. However, all signs point toward a more active pattern \non Sunday into early next week.",
